WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.852
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.020 --> 00:00:08.600
SPEAKER: BigQuery is a fully
managed cloud data warehouse

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.250
that provides
incredibly fast SQL

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.620
analytics over large datasets.

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.470
You can write and run queries
directly in the BigQuery web

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:16.400
UI.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:17.870
In this video,
you will learn how

00:00:17.870 --> 00:00:20.540
to use the BigQuery
UI to run a query.

00:00:20.540 --> 00:00:23.300
You will query the USA
Name Data public dataset

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:26.810
to determine the most common
names in the US between 1910

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.580
and 2013.

00:00:28.580 --> 00:00:31.730
This same process can be used
to query other public datasets

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:34.400
available through the
program, your own datasets,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.820
or any other dataset to which
you have the right permissions.

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:41.592
Get started by navigating to the
BigQuery UI at console.cloud.go

00:00:41.592 --> 00:00:45.650
ogle.com/bigquery.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.660
If you are new to
BigQuery, use the video

00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:49.550
linked in the description
below to set up

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:51.920
a new project in the
BigQuery sandbox.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.350
No credit card is required.

00:00:54.350 --> 00:00:57.440
If you are already a BigQuery
or Google Cloud platform user,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.050
you may select an
existing project.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.660
You will then land in the Query
Editor, where you can write

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.970
and run SQL queries directly.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:08.130
If the Query Editor is
not currently displayed,

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.650
click Compose New Query at
the top right of the window

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.270
to summon the editor.

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:14.040
Before writing a
query, you'll start

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.160
by navigating to the USA
Names public dataset.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.380
In the Resources section
of the left hand nav,

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:23.780
click Add Data
and Pin a Project.

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.660
Type bigquery-public-data,
separated by dashes,

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.910
and click Pin.

00:01:29.910 --> 00:01:32.940
This project contains
several public datasets.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:36.670
Expand the project and scroll
down and expand the USA Names

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:37.170
dataset.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.050
Click on the 1910
to Current table

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.150
where you can review
the table schema.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:48.240
The schema outlines the
structure of the table

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.210
and provides a list of the
columns available to query.

00:01:51.210 --> 00:01:54.630
Here, you can also view the
table details and preview

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.710
the data.

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.320
Click Query Table, and a
preloaded query statement

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.910
will appear in the
Query Editor, which

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.100
you can begin editing directly.

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:04.530
For this quick start,
instead of editing the query,

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.660
copy and paste the
query text provided

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.360
in the video description
below into the Query Editor,

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:10.890
and then click the
green checkmark

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.390
on the right hand
side of the window

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:14.630
to view the query validator.

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:16.570
A green checkmark
icon is displayed

00:02:16.570 --> 00:02:18.000
if the query is valid.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:21.090
If the query is invalid, a
red exclamation point icon

00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:22.320
is displayed.

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:24.000
The validator also
shows the amount

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:26.970
of data the query will
process when you run it.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:29.680
Now, click Run.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:32.950
The Query Results page displays
below the query window.

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.630
At the top of the
Query Results page,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:37.030
the time elapsed and the
data processed by the query

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:38.230
are displayed.

00:02:38.230 --> 00:02:40.840
Below, you'll see a table
displaying the query results

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.220
with a header row containing
the name of each column you

00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:45.810
selected in the query.

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:48.570
You may now choose to save the
query to be accessed and run

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.170
at a later time.

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:52.290
You may also save the
query results in one

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:54.900
of several formats
for further analysis,

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:57.240
or follow the direct link
to explore the results

00:02:57.240 --> 00:02:58.860
in Data Studio.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:00.030
Happy analyzing.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

